  "body": "Fork synced at v0.17.0 (branch cut from upstream/main f490458a).\n\n## What changed\n\nLaw (b)'s whole-move wall reading in Boundary mode (#43, XY arcs since #905) now covers XZ / YZ (G18 / G19) arcs too. Before, such an arc answered only at the union of its chord joints (#839).\n\n- New `crates/sim/src/verify/arc_wall_plane.rs` gives a vertical arc's XY footprint:\n  - **Planar** arc (normal coordinate equal at both ends): one exact segment along X (G18) or Y (G19), from the least to the greatest value the in-plane coordinate takes over the sweep. That is the endpoints, or `c +- r` where the sweep passes the coordinate's turn, read at the radius band's outer edge (`ARC_PAD`). It is read by the straight-move branch.\n  - **Helical** arc: a polyline of at most `MAX_ARC_CHORDS` pieces (sagitta 1e-4 mm), each piece read `r d^2 / 8` plus the band width nearer than it stands, so it never reads farther than the truth.\n  - Degenerate arcs (zero radius or sweep) read as the straight move, which is how `cl::arc_chords` cuts them. A non-finite centre or coordinate fails closed (`Trace::Unread`, a gouge). Nothing panics, and every loop is bounded.\n- `arc_wall.rs`: `Trace::of` always returns a trace, and the new `Trace::Poly` variant is added. `XyArc::dist_seg` uses a fixed 4-slot buffer instead of a `Vec` per edge (review note on #942).\n- `section.rs`: the wall reading runs for every non-rapid move in Boundary mode. Docs are updated.\n- `crates/app/tests/boundary_arc_walls.rs` moves to `crates/sim/tests/` because it uses only `verify` re-exports (#921). It is unchanged apart from the crate path.\n\n**Answer to the issue's P1 argument (it does not hold).** The footprint is a line, but the joints stand up to a chord length apart on it. On an r 200 arc at the 0.01 mm verdict tolerance, the pinned arc's joints (the union of both chord sets) are 1.92 mm apart in XY. A wall vertex 3.90 mm off the footprint, which is 90 um past a 4 mm cutter's 3.99 mm floor, read >= 4.016 mm from every joint, so the gouge passed. That is a real miss, and this PR fixes it.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `crates/sim/tests/boundary_arc_planes.rs` (new):\n  - `a_vertex_between_a_vertical_arcs_joints_past_the_floor_is_a_gouge`: G18 and G19 arcs, planar and helical (rise 0.2), cut both ways. The vertex is on both sides of the footprint at 3.90 / 3.5 / 3.8 / 3.98 mm. The test asserts that the joints read it clear (>= floor) and that the verdict is `Gouge`. **It fails without the fix**: I checked by stubbing the new trace to read nothing, which got `left: []`.\n  - `the_same_vertex_clear_of_the_floor_stays_clean`: the same cases at 3.995 / 4.0 / 4.5 mm (planar) and 4.0 / 4.5 / 6.0 mm (helical) give no warning. This checks for false refusals.\n  - `a_model_corner_between_a_vertical_arcs_joints_is_judged`: a model-backed pin. A 45-degree block corner gives a gouge at 3.90 mm and is clean at 4.5 mm. On this fixture the solid law (b2) also convicts the corner, so the test pins verdict and coverage of the model branch, not the wall reading alone.\n- Unit tests in `arc_wall_plane.rs`:\n  - `the_vertical_footprint_never_reads_farther_than_the_truth` compares against dense sampling of the arc. It covers planar and helical arcs in both planes, both directions, over and under a half turn, with 1600 random segments.\n  - `a_planar_arc_reads_out_to_its_turn`.\n  - `degenerate_vertical_arcs_are_read_safely`: zero-length/zero-sweep arcs, a NaN centre, and an r 1e9 helix capped at 1024 pieces.\n- Existing pins: `boundary_arc_walls` (3 tests, now in sim) and `arc_wall::the_exact_distance_matches_a_dense_sampling` still pass.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-sim --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean.\n- Quick gate (`cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`): green, 4.0 min (sim 10 s, motion 5 s, app 225 s; 0 failed).\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No golden moved, and no generator in the tree emits a helical XZ / YZ arc today. The helical branch is covered by the unit and end-to-end fixtures only.\n- In the no-model case, a polyline crossing test (`crosses`) on a helical footprint is approximate at the pad scale (~0.1 um). This matches what straight chords did before.\n- Area mode is unchanged: it already read every chord whole.\n\nChangelog: Boundary-mode gouge check now measures XZ / YZ (G18 / G19) arcs along their whole XY footprint, so a wall corner between two chord joints is no longer missed (#946)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #946\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
